# Stack-native architecture

Apply this rule only after detecting the installed stack from repository
evidence and reading applicable project contracts and ADRs. A familiar
stack is not the default. If detection is uncertain, say so and do not
apply an unrelated profile.

Canonical check, exceptions, and baseline behavior:
`docs/contracts/features/stack-native-architecture.md`. Decision:
ADR 0011.

Appearance, design references, and visual fidelity do not waive this
rule. Implement the approved design with the stack's normal boundaries.

## Template and MVC-style views

Use this profile for Blade and comparable server templates when the
file's package is Laravel, Roots/Sage/Acorn, or another MVC-style
template architecture. Choose that package from the file's ancestor
Composer manifest. A sibling package does not apply, and a nested
`package.json` does not hide the Composer framework. Use that
framework's own layer names. A PHP view with no such package is
uncertain: say so, and do not apply Roots field rules to it.

- Views render prepared data.
- Conditions, loops, formatting, components, and class selection may
  live in the template.
- Queries, persistence, business decisions, substantial data
  transformation, content parsing, and service orchestration belong in
  the controller, composer, presenter, service, or equivalent.
- Do not treat a template expression or helper call as prohibited logic
  by itself.
- Do not impose Laravel controllers, actions, or service class names on
  a framework that has a different structure.

Roots/Sage/Acorn: prepare fields and queries in composers or the
equivalent Acorn layer. `get_field` and `WP_Query` in a new Blade view
are not the presentation layer.

## Components

Use this profile for React, Vue, Svelte, Solid, Preact, Angular, and
similar component architectures.

- Follow that framework's component, hook, rendering, and data-access
  conventions.
- Do not apply MVC "no logic in views" restrictions to components.
- Do not invent a shared separation that conflicts with the installed
  framework.

A mixed repository can use the template profile for Blade and the
component profile for React. Do not collapse them into one rule.

## Styling

Use the Tailwind profile only when Tailwind is the styling system for
the file you are changing. Detection may differ by package or directory.
A root Tailwind dependency does not cover a Bootstrap, CSS Modules, or
CSS-in-JS package.

When Tailwind applies, follow `20-coding`:

- Compose with native utilities and project theme tokens.
- CSS may contain framework imports, theme definitions, font faces, and
  CSS that utilities cannot reasonably express.
- Do not add a semantic class, custom declaration, or `@apply` wrapper
  to relocate utility-supported styling.
- Do not use an arbitrary utility when a standard utility or token works.
- A legitimate exception names the concrete limitation and stays on the
  finding: `guardrail-allow-architecture: ...`

When the file uses plain CSS, Bootstrap, CSS Modules, CSS-in-JS, or
another system, follow that system. Do not forbid its stylesheet and do
not rewrite it into Tailwind utilities.

WordPress with Tailwind and `theme.json` still follows
`36-wordpress-theme.mdc` for presets and editorial styles.

## Enforcement

Run `scripts/check-guardrails.sh` when the task changes public-facing
copy, templates, components, or styling. The command reports profiles,
checks, and coverage gaps. Fix blocking findings, or record a narrow
exception with a concrete limitation.

Do not add `guardrail-allow-architecture`, `guardrail-allow-file:
architecture`, or a `docs/architecture-baseline.txt` entry to make the
command pass. File-level architecture exemptions are invalid. A baseline
is an approved inventory of existing committed findings, not a way to
hide new work.

Lint, tests, build, and a passing check do not prove architecture
compliance. When views, components, data preparation, or styling change,
the completion includes the short architecture review defined in the
contract: profiles, mechanical result, and the boundaries the check
cannot judge.

If a project contract or ADR intentionally conflicts with this rule,
stop and report the conflict. Do not overwrite that decision.
